# ---------------- Lessons ----------------
LESSONS += [
    {
        'id': 'model-selection-effort-caching-and-cost',
        'title': 'Model Selection, Effort, Caching and Cost Control',
        'summary': 'Tokens, context windows, sampling and next-token generation; thinking, effort and fast mode; Opus, Sonnet, Haiku and Fable tradeoffs and migration breaking changes; token budgeting, cost modeling, prompt caching and batch processing. Covers Domain 5: LLM Fundamentals, Technical Fundamentals, Model Selection and Tradeoffs, and Cost and Token Management.',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f1001', 'f1003', 'f1009', 'f1011', 'f1013', 'f1019', 'f1021', 'f1027', 'f1029'],
        'quizIds': ['q1103', 'q1114', 'q1116', 'q1118', 'q1122', 'q1124'],
        'reading': (
            'Everything starts with tokens: small chunks of text, often word fragments, in which prices, rate limits and the context window are measured. Claude 4.7 and later models split the same text into about 30 percent more tokens than older ones, so estimates must be recounted for the target model. A model writes one token at a time, so after the first token the wait grows with output length; asking for three sentences instead of a memo is often the biggest latency win. The context window holds input and reply together, thinking included, and caching makes repeated input cheaper without shrinking it. Output varies between calls, and current models reject non-default temperature, top_p and top_k with a 400, so tests check properties, never exact strings.\n\n'
            'Pick the tier by the work. Haiku 5.5 is fastest and cheapest, for classification, extraction, routing and subagents. Sonnet 5.5 balances speed and intelligence for everyday coding and agents. Opus 5.5 is the documented starting point for long-running agentic coding and knowledge work. Fable 5.1 is the most capable model open to all, slower and dearest; as a Covered Model it needs 30-day data retention, so zero retention is unavailable by default. Start efficiency-first (begin small, move up where tests show gaps) or capability-first (begin strong, then trade down while evals still pass). Either way, decide on an evaluation set built from your own prompts and compare cost per completed task, since retries can make the cheaper token price the dearer outcome.\n\n'
            'Thinking depth is a dial. Adaptive thinking lets the model decide whether and how long to reason; it is always on for Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1, and the old budget_tokens mode returns a 400. Effort, from low to max, is a soft signal that scales reasoning, tool calls and answer length together, and tuning effort often beats switching models. Thinking is billed as output and counts toward max_tokens, the hard ceiling, so leave room. Fast mode on some Opus models gives up to about 2.5 times the output speed for a premium, but not a quicker first token. Before moving to a new release, read its migration notes and run evals: prefill, forced tool_choice and thinking disabled are rejected on newer models.\n\n'
            'Money follows tokens. Output costs about five times input, and an agent that resends its history each turn pays for it again every turn. Prompt caching is usually the biggest free win. The prefix runs tools, system, then messages; put a breakpoint (up to four) on the last block that stays identical, never on a timestamp. A five-minute write costs about 1.25 times normal input and a read a tenth or less, so a 10,000-token prefix sent 11 times in five minutes costs about a fifth of the uncached price. Use the one-hour lifetime when users pause 8 to 40 minutes. Editing tools or the system prompt, or changing effort or tool_choice, invalidates the cache; the usage fields show whether it worked. Work that can wait a day goes to the Batch API at half price, stacking with caching.\n\n'
            'Official SDKs wrap the REST API and add headers, retries, timeouts and streaming helpers. Replies stream over server-sent events, not WebSockets, which belong between your backend and the browser. Read capabilities from the Models API instead of hard-coding model names.'
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to model selection? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Choosing a model is like choosing a vehicle for deliveries. A scooter (Haiku) is cheap and quick for small parcels across town, a van (Sonnet) handles most routes, a truck (Opus) hauls the big, long jobs, and a heavy-haul rig (Fable) is for the loads nothing else can move. Effort is how carefully the driver plans the route: more planning costs time and fuel, and on an easy trip it is wasted. Prompt caching is leaving the standard boxes loaded on the vehicle between trips, and the Batch API is the overnight freight service: slower, but half the price. You decide with test deliveries on your own roads, not from the brochure.',
        'keyTerms': ['Token and tokenizer', 'Next-token generation', 'Adaptive thinking', 'Effort', 'Fast mode', 'Cost per completed task', 'Prompt caching', 'Cache invalidation', 'Message Batches API'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'Treating temperature 0 as a determinism switch. Current models reject non-default sampling values and no setting makes output byte-identical, so tests must check properties rather than exact text.',
            'Comparing models by price per million tokens instead of cost per completed task on your own evals; retries, extra turns and failures change the answer.',
            'Capping max_tokens to save money. It truncates (and still bills) rather than making the model economize, and at high effort hidden thinking can use the whole allowance; control cost with effort and a request for brevity.',
            'Assuming caching shrinks the context window, or that any prompt gets cached. It changes price only, needs a stable prefix of at least the minimum length, and any edit before the breakpoint or a change of effort or tool_choice invalidates it.',
            'Carrying settings across releases. Manual budget_tokens, prefill, forced tool_choice, thinking disabled and non-default sampling fail with a 400 on newer models, and the new tokenizer changes token counts.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A travel company runs three Claude features. A fare-rule classifier handles 30 million short messages a month and must answer in under a second, so the team starts with Haiku 5.5, scores it on a labeled sample and keeps it. A trip-planning agent runs for an hour with many tool calls; it starts on Opus 5.5, sweeps effort downward on its eval set and keeps the cheapest level that still passes. Both resend a 12,000-token policy prefix, so the team puts a cache breakpoint on the last stable block, moves the timestamp after it, uses the one-hour lifetime for the agent whose users pause, and sends a nightly quality run through the Batch API.',
        'onTheJob': 'Build the eval set before arguing about models, and re-run it whenever the model, effort, tools or system prompt change. Log the usage object for every call, including the cache fields, and watch the cache-read share: a sudden drop usually means someone put a timestamp or reordered tools in the prefix. Set max_tokens generously and treat max_tokens stops as failures to handle. Pin full model IDs in production, read migration notes before upgrading, and recount tokens for a new tokenizer. Give each feature and environment its own workspace so the Usage and Cost API shows where the money goes.',
    },
    {
        'id': 'testing-debugging-and-claude-code-operation',
        'title': 'Evals, Debugging and Claude Code Operation',
        'summary': 'Designing evals, choosing graders and running regressions; identifying error types and silent failures, choosing recovery strategies, analyzing traces and isolating whether the integration or the model is at fault; and operating Claude Code: CLAUDE.md placement, rules, commands, subagents and memory, sessions, headless output and permission modes. Covers Domain 4 (Debugging and Error Handling) and Domain 3 (Claude Code Operation).',
        'diagram': None,
        'vocabIds': ['f1202', 'f1205', 'f1207', 'f1208', 'f1209', 'f1212', 'f1215', 'f1216', 'f1218'],
        'quizIds': ['q1129', 'q1130', 'q1132', 'q1134', 'q1135', 'q1137'],
        'reading': (
            'You cannot tune what you cannot measure. Start with success criteria that are specific, measurable, achievable and relevant, such as 95 percent of invoice totals exact. Build an evaluation set that mirrors real traffic and includes edge cases: irrelevant, overlong, ambiguous or harmful inputs. Use the fastest reliable grader: code-based grading for checkable answers, an LLM judge with a detailed rubric and a constrained verdict for tone and completeness (tested against human labels first), and humans sparingly. Re-run the whole set after every prompt, tool or model change and compare with the baseline; that regression run catches the fix that broke something else.\n\n'
            'Debugging starts with naming the failure. Transport and request errors raise exceptions: 429, 5xx and 529 are retried with backoff, while 400, 401 and 403 need a code or configuration fix. The subtle class is the silent failure, an HTTP 200 with a bad result: a reply cut off at max_tokens, a refusal, output that breaks the schema, or a confident wrong answer. Only stop_reason checks and validation in code see these. Match the recovery to the cause: retry transient faults, correct and resend invalid requests, continue after truncation, feed a validation error back for one bounded second attempt, and fall back or escalate when it persists.\n\n'
            'When behavior is wrong, read evidence instead of guessing. A trace is the full record of one request and a span is one step in it, such as a model call; the Claude API and SDK can export them with OpenTelemetry, by default with metadata only. Failing traces reveal patterns such as a repeated search. To tell your integration from the model, capture the exact request the app sent and replay it on its own. If the replay is fine, suspect history assembly, headers, truncation, parsing or tool-result formatting; if it fails the same way, work on the prompt, tool descriptions, model or validation. For cache misses, cache diagnostics reports where consecutive requests first differed.\n\n'
            'Claude Code offers several homes for guidance. CLAUDE.md files (managed, user, project, local) are concatenated into context at launch; /init drafts one from your codebase or suggests improvements to an existing one. Keep it short and move topic conventions into .claude/rules files with a paths pattern, which load only for matching files. Reusable procedures live in skills or command files; helpers with their own context live in .claude/agents files, whose memory setting (user, project or local) decides where their notes persist. Auto memory is written by Claude, on your machine. All of this is context, not enforcement; what must always hold belongs in a hook or permission rule.\n\n'
            'For sessions, /clear starts fresh, /compact summarizes, --continue or --resume returns, and /branch (or --fork-session) copies a conversation so the original stays intact. In scripts, claude -p runs headless: --output-format json returns one object (add --json-schema for a structured_output field), stream-json emits one event per line, and --bare skips local configuration so CI runners behave alike. Permission modes run from asking, through acceptEdits and plan, to auto mode, where a classifier reviews actions; bypassPermissions is for isolated sandboxes only.'
        ),
        'fundamentalsLabel': 'New to evals and debugging? See the everyday analogy',
        'fundamentals': 'Evals are the driving test for your prompt: a fixed route with known hazards that every new version must pass before it is allowed on the road. Debugging a failed run is accident investigation. You do not repeat the journey from memory; you read the black-box recording (the trace) and replay the exact conditions on a test track (the isolated request) to learn whether the car, the road or the driver was at fault. Claude Code\'s configuration is the workshop: a posted rulebook (CLAUDE.md), drawers that open only for matching jobs (path rules and skills), and a locked gate (hooks and permissions) for what must never happen.',
        'keyTerms': ['Success criteria', 'Evaluation set', 'Silent failure', 'Recovery strategy selection', 'Trace analysis', 'Problem origin isolation', 'Path-scoped rules', 'Structured headless output', 'Auto mode'],
        'commonTraps': [
            'Retrying every failure. A 400, a refusal or a schema error fails the same way each time, so match the recovery to the cause and cap the attempts.',
            'Trusting HTTP 200. Truncated, refused or malformed replies are successful responses; check stop_reason and validate the output in code.',
            'Blaming the model first. When the same prompt works elsewhere, replay the exact logged request to learn whether your integration changed it.',
            'Judging a prompt change on three hand-picked examples, or building an eval set from clean inputs only; real regressions hide in the cases you did not look at.',
            'Treating CLAUDE.md, rules or auto memory as enforcement. They are context; a rule that must hold needs a hook or permission rule, and auto memory is machine-local while the committed CLAUDE.md is the shared file.',
        ],
        'scenario': 'A claims-intake service starts rejecting about 4 percent of its model replies as invalid JSON after a Friday deploy. The on-call engineer does not touch the prompt. She pulls logged request bodies from before and after the deploy, sees that the new code sets max_tokens to 400 instead of 4,000, and confirms from the raw responses that the failing calls ended with stop_reason max_tokens. She restores the limit, adds a check that treats max_tokens as a failure and retries once with a larger ceiling, and adds the failing inputs to the eval set. Meanwhile the team trims its CLAUDE.md, moves the claims-schema conventions into a path-scoped rule, and has CI call claude -p with --output-format json and a schema.',
        'onTheJob': 'Log the request ID, model, stop_reason and usage for every call, and keep the exact request body for failures, so a replay is possible a week later. Decide per failure type what the app does (retry, repair, fall back, escalate) and test those paths with injected faults. Add every production failure to the eval set. Keep trace content modes in line with your privacy rules: metadata only is the safe default, full content is for debugging with retention you control. In Claude Code, trim what /init writes before committing it, keep CLAUDE.md short, move situational guidance into rules and skills, and use --bare in CI so every runner behaves the same.',
    },
]


